WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.936
Container images package an application
with the files, dependencies,

2
00:00:07.016 --> 00:00:09.653
and metadata needed to run it.

3
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:15.536
Most images start from a base image, then
add layers for packages,

4
00:00:15.616 --> 00:00:20.469
application code, configuration, and
runtime setup.

5
00:00:20.549 --> 00:00:23.503
Each layer reflects a build step,

6
00:00:23.583 --> 00:00:30.652
and those layers can contain useful review
clues such as package installation,

7
00:00:30.732 --> 00:00:36.746
copied files, environment settings, or
leftover build artifacts.

8
00:00:36.826 --> 00:00:39.674
Tags and digests are important.

9
00:00:39.754 --> 00:00:44.397
A tag such as latest or production may
move over time,

10
00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:51.440
while a digest identifies a specific
immutable image version more precisely.

11
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:58.694
Reviews should prefer evidence that points
to the exact image under discussion.

12
00:00:58.774 --> 00:01:03.733
If the report references a tag but the tag
later changes,

13
00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:06.978
the evidence may become ambiguous.

14
00:01:07.058 --> 00:01:10.645
Digests help make findings repeatable.

15
00:01:10.725 --> 00:01:14.312
Registries and provenance also matter.

16
00:01:14.392 --> 00:01:21.250
The reviewer should understand where
images come from, who can publish them,

17
00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:25.022
whether signing or verification is used,

18
00:01:25.102 --> 00:01:28.901
how images move through promotion stages,

19
00:01:28.981 --> 00:01:33.623
and whether base images are trusted and
maintained.

20
00:01:33.703 --> 00:01:40.877
An SBOM can help identify software
components and dependencies inside an image,

21
00:01:40.957 --> 00:01:47.709
but it should be interpreted with the same
care as any other evidence source.

22
00:01:47.789 --> 00:01:53.697
AI can help explain image structure and
summarize scanner output,

23
00:01:53.777 --> 00:01:56.943
especially when reports are large.

24
00:01:57.023 --> 00:02:03.564
It can group findings by package, layer,
base image, or remediation path.

25
00:02:03.644 --> 00:02:08.180
The reviewer should still verify scanner
results,

26
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:12.586
check whether the image matches the
deployment,

27
00:02:12.666 --> 00:02:19.524
and understand whether the vulnerable
component is actually present, used,

28
00:02:19.604 --> 00:02:22.980
or reachable in the workload context.
